 usually comes from the plumbing rather than the source \u2014 most often from copper pipe and its fittings, especially where water sits still.","effect":"Copper is an essential nutrient, but some people who drink water containing copper in excess of the action level over a relatively short amount of time could experience gastrointestinal distress. Some people who drink water containing copper in excess of the action level over many years could suffer liver or kidney damage.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"People with Wilson's disease should consult their doctor.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Copper"},"Antimony, Total":{"plain":"Antimony reaches water from petroleum refineries, fire retardants, ceramics, electronics and solder.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing antimony well in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years could experience increases in blood cholesterol and decreases in blood sugar.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Antimony","who":"Long-term exposure.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Antimony, Total"},"Lead":{"plain":"Lead is almost never in water at its source. It gets picked up on the way to the tap \u2014 from the service line under the street, from solder in older joints, from the brass in the tap itself. That makes it a property of your building more than of your town.","effect":"Infants and children who drink water containing lead in excess of the action level could experience delays in their physical or mental development. Children could show slight deficits in attention span and learning abilities. Adults who drink this water over many years could develop kidney problems or high blood pressure.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Lead and Copper Rule","who":"Children and infants first.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Lead"},"Lead and Copper Rule":{"kind":"rule","name":"Lead and Copper Rule","plain":"The rule governing lead and copper monitoring and corrosion control. A violation is often procedural \u2014 a missed sampling round or a late report.","effect":null,"source":null,"who":null},"Fluoride":{"plain":"Fluoride occurs naturally in some groundwater and is added deliberately by many systems to reduce tooth decay. A violation usually means too much, not too little.","effect":"Some people who drink water containing fluoride in excess of the maximum contaminant level over many years could get bone disease, including pain and tenderness of the bones. Children may get mottled teeth.","source":"U.S. EPA, mandatory health effects language \u2014 Fluoride","who":"Children, for tooth mottling.","kind":"contaminant","name":"Fluoride"}}}